A||||12||Alex4mula||Help on 3.4 install||||5||
Z||000000||Alex4mula||05-05-2000||09:03 AM||||Well, it is the time to start this project. Will try to post pictures and experiences. It is for an 88 Formula 5 speed. Project involves installation of 3.4 (from Ed Parks), with headers (Focoa), under drive pulleys, resurface flywheel, new Kevlar clutch (Fieroshop) fuel preassure reg, porting upper manifolds, all new mounts and some detailing.<BR>First I was going to try to pull the engine from the top and I am still considering it. Ed told me that it would be easier to drop the cradle. My question is, How hard it really is to drop the cradle in this car? How much time? I know I may need a dolly, but how high I may need to lift the car to clear the engine without the top mflds? Apart from the cradle bolts, shock tower bolts and engine wiring, piping, etc., what else may need to be disconnected from the suspension? Do I need to disconnect the sway bar? Any area to be specially carefull? Should I keep my plan to pull it from the top? I wouldn't like to disconect any axle if necesary. All help and comments will be greatly appreciated. Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||05-05-2000||11:50 AM||||I've performed two clutch changes on my '88 Formula 5-speed. Both times I lowered the cradle. Not very difficult at all. When the time comes to order a 3.4 I plan to drop the cradle. This is all from memory, don't quote me on this setup.<BR>disconnect battery any pull it out. There are two fuel lines to disconnect just to the right and below the plenum. Then disconnect select and shift cables from tranny(just hang them out of the way). Slave cylinder is on a bracet, leave cylinder on bracket but disconnect bracket from tranny(no need to bleed clutch system this way). <P><BR>More to come, gotta' go.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||05-05-2000||11:58 AM||||Alex, <BR>Once you disconnect the struts at the top, the *entire* suspension should come out with the cradle. Don't forget your AC and fuel lines. I've heard it's easier to unbolt the compressor from the engine, and leave it in the car with the lines connected. Will save you an AC recharge. <BR>It's gonna be real tight to pull it out the top. I would drop the cradle. If you have a big beam in your garage, and a come-a-long, you can lift the whole back of the car up off of the cradle. One guy I know uses a chain with two hooks. Catches the hooks in the holes at the top of the shock towers.<BR>He has never done an 88, but it should be similar. <BR>You should have hung around till Sunday. We buzzards pulled the engine out of Jason's 86 "parts GT". Was a tremendous learning experience. <BR>Count on it taking about four times as long as your first estimate.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||triker||05-05-2000||01:00 PM||||Alex, drop the cradle.  If you have a beam in your garage, possition the back window under it.  Slip an 8' 4x4 under the car in front of the rear wheels.  Use a hoist or come-a-long on each side of the car from the 4x4 to the beam.  You can pick the car as high as you need and what is great about it is when you go back, the car is kind of floating and you can push it around to line up the cradle bolts.  You'll also want a dolly about 4 inches high.  You can build one out of a piece of 3/4 plywood and some casters from the hardware store.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||05-05-2000||01:25 PM||||OK.. You all finally convinced me. I have droped the rear cradle (trunk side) rocking it to do the clutch job. So it will be some more items to disconnect. I have a nice hoist which I may use to lift the car. <BR>Questions: I guess I should have the car lifted, then lower the cradle (once disconnected) with the hoist into a dolly, and then try to lift the car with the hoist? Could it be easier (less height) if I try to roll the cradle getting the engine out through the wheel opening? <P>Rydar, I whish I could have stayed to see that but I needed to drive 900 miles back on Sunday to work Monday. Hope next year take my 3.4 there :-)<P>Thank you all.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||05-05-2000||02:49 PM||||Hey Alex,<P>When I pulled the mill out of my GT, I built a dolly out of 2x4's and some 4" casters. I built it to size for the cradle. I stripped the motor while it was inside the car. All harnesses, hoses, etc, right down to the bone. Then I jacked the car up, pulled the tires, and removed the nuts from the front cradle bolts, (they sit horozontally) and loosened the bolts to the back mounts, then I gently lowered the whole assembly on the dolly and removed the cradle bolts. Viola! The body lifted right up off the cradle slick as can be! Took me about a half a day by myself. Saves alot of jacking and trying to support the cradle, etc.....<BR>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||05-05-2000||03:13 PM||||I used a four point chasis lift and a fork-lift to hold the cradle. Having the right tools makes a job so much easier!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Alex4mula||05-05-2000||06:06 PM||||Thank you all. Tomorrow I'll get the grease in my hands. My goal is to take the engine out this weekend and start the teardown/cleanning process. Let's see how it goes... Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Alex4mula||05-05-2000||07:44 PM||||Oh well... I took a look under there for a final assessment and found this;<P>The brake lines will need to be disconnected which will require later re-bleeding. Well, that's not that bad. But also found that the emergency brake line is in the middle connected to both cradle and frame.<BR>So I guess this needs to be disconnected too. Never done that and looks kind of difficult. Is it as hard as it looks? Any easier way?? Tips? Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000009||dman||05-05-2000||10:30 PM||||Hi Alex4mula. In your last post you stated that the "brake lines will need to be disconnected which will require later re-bleeding".<P>If you are going to drop the cradle, you don't need to disconnect the brakes. Just unbolt the calipers (a couple of caliper slider bolts), unbolt the holdown clamp that holds the brake line against the strut, this way you can then hang the calipers off to the side with some wire (don't let them hang by the brake lines alone!).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Alex4mula||05-05-2000||10:32 PM||||?? ??||||reg||5
Z||000011||LarryB||05-06-2000||05:58 AM||||dman's got it right, just remove the calipers and gently put them aside, bend up a wire coathanger to help hang and support them.  If you have a Chiltons or == it should describe how to remove them.<P>pull your wheel off and slide your hand along the front of the caliper to the back side and up and down, near the top and bottom on the inside you'll feel a couple of what feel almost like rubber gaskets sticking out, inside the 'gaskets' you'll feel bolt heads, you remove those 2 bolts and use a pair of pliers to pull up and out the parking brake cable.  Just be careful when removing the caliper so you don't pop the pads off their mounts.  If the car is properly and safely supported you can see what you're doing by sliding underneath the car with a trouble light. <P>Those 2 screws have Torx heads, from memory they're T-25 but you need to check that. Do NOT even attempt to use a large Allen wrench, at best you'll damage the head, at worst you'll strip it without loosening the bolt. Definitely a situation where using the right tool will keep your blood pressure down.  Replacement bolts are about $10 each and removing one whose head you ruined doesn't make for a fun day, I learned this the hard way when I did the brakes a few years ago. Between this info, a shop manual, and the right tools you shouldn't have any serious trouble.<P>Putting the calipers back on is just the reverse of taking them off, a good pair of needlenose pliers helps you pull the parking brake cable enough to slip it back into the notch on the bracket, expect to need a few tries with a couple of strings of cursing Pontiac engineers thrown in to make you feel a bit better.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Alex4mula||05-06-2000||06:42 PM||||Thankks Larry & dman. I  have removed the calipers before. Is just that seems so easy to just unplug that instead of the calipers. I guess that at the end with the bleeding it will take the same time. I'll just remove them. <BR>** What still worries me is the emergency brake cable. I took another look and seems messy to remove. Anyone have removed this one before? Is it very hard? Any trick? Tks.||||reg||1
